 "protocol": {
  "summary": "Hydraulic machine: actuator moves up so a roller on the upper cup contacts a horizontal plate at offset d (~30 mm), flexing the segment while compressing it (peak compression 476-1795 N, mean 925 N at the elastic limit); each load/unload cycle 2.0 s; flexion increased in 1 deg steps until residual deformation; the curve 1 deg below was analysed; compression curve (d=0) subtracted; moment = F sin(theta) sqrt(d^2+h^2).",
  "how_to_simulate": [
   "Fix the lower vertebra of the lumbar segment (all posterior elements intact) at the reported level",
   "Flex the upper vertebra about the disc centre with a combined load: vertical force applied at ~30 mm anterior offset so that compression rises with flexion (~925 N at the elastic limit), 1 s loading",
   "Take the model flexion angle at the elastic limit as the measured mean range of flexion for the level (e.g. 10.5 deg at L4-L5) or the model's own failure onset",
   "Output the flexion moment about the disc centre at 10-90% of that angle, normalise by the moment at 100%, and compare; also compare absolute max BM (Nm)"
  ],
  "subject_model": null,
  "notes": "In vivo bending moments in the same paper are derived from these curves (not measured) and were not extracted. Composite whole-lumbar curve (Fig 6) is a weighted construct and not extracted. Moment includes the compressive-load contribution about the disc centre."
 },
